# CODEOWNERS: safety-gated code and the machinery that enforces it.
#
# Scope: the "Safety-gated code" list in CONTRIBUTING.md "Safety review
# process" (D-013, SECURITY.md Authorization), plus the files that enforce
# the rules: lint config, dependency/global/write-gating checks, CI, and this
# file. There is deliberately no catch-all `*` line: other paths have no code
# owner and need only the normal review.
#
# How the "second approval" is enforced (US-RR-8, US-RR-8-7):
# - This file only takes effect when branch protection has "Require review
#   from Code Owners" (require_code_owner_reviews) switched on. GitHub reads
#   CODEOWNERS from the PR's *base* branch, so it must be committed on every
#   protected branch (master and rust-rewrite).
# - GitHub cannot vary the required approval count per path. The chosen setup
#   is "Required approving reviews: 1" + "Require review from Code Owners" +
#   "Require approval of the most recent reviewable push". A PR author can
#   never approve their own PR, so a PR touching a path below needs an
#   approval from a code owner who is not the author: the author plus a
#   second, owning reviewer. Other paths need one approval from anyone.
# - Limit, stated plainly: with a single code owner, a PR the owner authors
#   on these paths cannot get a code-owner approval from anyone else. Until a
#   second maintainer is listed here, such PRs need an admin bypass (which
#   is visible in the PR timeline). For a strict two-approval rule, set
#   "Required approving reviews: 2"; that then applies to every PR.
# - Patterns are file-based. Write code added to an unowned file (e.g. a
#   crate's lib.rs) is caught by the T6 check (`cargo xtask check-writes`),
#   not by this file.
# The exact branch-protection command is in CONTRIBUTING.md, "Branch
# protection (maintainers)".
#
# Syntax: gitignore-style patterns, a leading `/` anchors at the repo root,
# and the LAST matching line wins. Paths that do not exist yet are listed so
# they are owned from their first commit.

